# Security headers for every file the site serves (Cloudflare reads this file from the built
# site: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md section 8, "Security"). The API's answers set their own
# (worker/http.ts).
#
# - Content-Security-Policy: scripts, styles, pictures, sounds and requests come from this
#   site only, the bot check's script and frame from Cloudflare's challenges.cloudflare.com
#   (Turnstile), and Cloudflare Web Analytics' script and its count of the visit
#   (static.cloudflareinsights.com, cloudflareinsights.com: the product owner keeps it on,
#   2026-11-18), and Google AdSense's ads (the product owner, 2026-11-19: "i want to have
#   ads"): its scripts, its ads' frames and pictures, and its requests, from Google's ad
#   domains (googlesyndication.com, doubleclick.net, google.com, gstatic.com,
#   adtrafficquality.google, googletagservices.com, googleadservices.com) and its consent
#   message's fonts (fonts.googleapis.com, fonts.gstatic.com). No plugins, no other base address, no form posted anywhere else, and no other
#   site may show these pages in a frame. 'unsafe-inline' is there because the pages are built
#   ahead of time with their start-up scripts and their styles written into them
#   (tools/inline-css.ts).
# - Strict-Transport-Security: browsers only ever come back over HTTPS (a year).
# - The rest: no guessing of file types, no framing, the address sent onwards cut to the site
#   alone, no camera, microphone, location or payments, and a window of its own.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://challenges.cloudflare.com https://static.cloudflareinsights.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.doubleclick.net https://*.google.com https://*.adtrafficquality.google https://*.googletagservices.com https://*.googleadservices.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.doubleclick.net https://*.google.com https://*.adtrafficquality.google https://*.gstatic.com; font-src 'self' data: https://fonts.gstatic.com; media-src 'self' data: blob:; connect-src 'self' https://cloudflareinsights.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.doubleclick.net https://*.google.com https://*.adtrafficquality.google; frame-src https://challenges.cloudflare.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.doubleclick.net https://*.google.com https://*.adtrafficquality.google; worker-src 'self' blob:; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin

# How long a browser may keep a file (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, "Hosting"). Without a rule
# Cloudflare sends "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate": kept, but asked about on every
# visit. Everything the build writes under /assets (the scripts, the stylesheet, the fonts)
# has its contents' hash in its name, so a name never means other contents: kept for thirty
# days and not asked about meanwhile. A returning player downloads none of it.
#
# Thirty days and not the usual "a year, immutable", because Cloudflare puts a rule's headers
# on every answer for a matching address, a "404 Not Found" too. While a deploy spreads, a
# page of the new build can ask a server still on the old one for a file the old one has not
# got, and the browser would keep that "not found" for as long as this says, for a file
# that then exists. A month bounds that; a player who comes every day is served from their
# own copy either way, and PageSpeed's "efficient cache lifetimes" asks for thirty days.
#
# Nothing else has a rule, on purpose. The pages (the HTML) must be asked about every visit,
# or a deploy would not show. The files whose names stay the same when their contents change
# (/sounds/*, the favicon, the link preview's picture) are asked about too: a "not changed"
# costs a few hundred bytes, after the page is up, and a new recording plays at once.
#
# Only ONE rule that matches an address may set Cache-Control: a second is joined to the
# first with a comma (the rule for every path, above, sets none).
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000
